<header><p class="st-kicker">prince bishopric · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Prince-Provostry of Ellwangen</h1><p class="st-meta">1460 CE – 1803 CE · capital Ellwangen</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Benedictine abbey on the Jagst that was converted in 1460 into a provostry of noble canons whose princes ruled a sizeable Swabian territory.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ellwangen">Ellwangen</a> abbey was founded around 764 by Hariolf, later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/langres">Langres</a>, and received Carolingian protection. It built up lands along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagst">Jagst</a>, and its abbots held princely rank from the 13th century. In 1460 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-ii">Pope Pius II</a> converted the abbey into a secular collegiate foundation headed by a prince-provost.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The prince-provost, often a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> elsewhere as well, governed a considerable territory around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ellwangen">Ellwangen</a> with his own courts and officials and sat among the ecclesiastical princes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-diet">Imperial Diet</a>. The castle above the town was his residence. Witch trials between 1611 and 1618 were among the most severe in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, claiming several hundred victims.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1802 and 1803 the provostry was secularised and annexed by Württemberg, which for some years made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ellwangen">Ellwangen</a> the seat of the government of its new Catholic lands. The castle and the Romanesque collegiate basilica survive.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/prince-provostry-of-ellwangen.jpg" alt="Prince-Provostry of Ellwangen" width="320" height="244"><figcaption>Image: AlexanderRahm · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ellwangen_Kirche_3795.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1460 CE – 1803 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>prince bishopric</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Ellwangen</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Swabian German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Germany</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Fürstpropstei Ellwangen</dd></dl></aside></div>
